83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (One evening not long after returning to Kisiizi, I left the front door of my little house open. A few minutes later, I looked up to find a goat standing inside!)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (This was not particularly unusual by Kisiizi standards, but it did make me laugh. Apparently, after being away for seven months, I had forgotten one of the important rules of life here: if you leave the door open, you should probably expect visitors.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (Since coming back, that has been true in more ways than one.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (My house has become a place where people come and go. A baby who was abandoned and is now being cared for by the hospital spends many of my days off with me. Neighbours stop by. Friends from fellowship groups come to visit. Colleagues finishing a late shift at the hospital sometimes arrive hungry and decide that my kitchen is easier than going home to cook. And apparently, this invitation extends to goats as well.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (It is one of the things I love about life here. There is a sense that you do not have to make an appointment to be part of one another's lives. People simply show up.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (And after seven months away, it has been good to have people showing up again. To be welcomed back so warmly into a community whose language and culture I am still learning to understand. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
