149 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (One of the things God has been showing me since coming back is that if I want to pour into other people, I also need to learn how to be filled.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Life here can be quite full. My mornings often begin with chapel before heading into clinical work in the Emergency Department. In between patients and shifts, there are research projects to help with, children to care for, Sunday school to prepare, meals to cook, people to visit, and all the ordinary things that somehow fill up the hours of a day. I enjoy being busy. I enjoy having people around. I enjoy feeling useful.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
151 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (But I am learning that I cannot continually pour out without first taking time to be filled myself. Jesus Himself withdrew to quiet places. He prayed. He spent time with His Father. If I want to keep giving to the people around me, I need to learn to do the same.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Sometimes it means taking a nap in the afternoon because I am tired. Sometimes it means sitting on my front steps with a novel and letting the to-do list wait. Sometimes it means going for a walk simply because it is beautiful outside. And sometimes it means taking a little trip into town to stay with a friend, eating ice cream, and remembering that life is not only about getting things done.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
